<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>MMD as Linear Classification Risk</h3>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we expand on the discussion in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 MMD and its Interpretation as Classification Risk ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and derive how MMD may be seen as the optimal risk for distinguishing between <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> of a binary classifier in the reproducing kernel Hilbert space <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Using the notation <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x}[k(x,\;\cdot\;)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.280em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x}[k(x,\;\cdot\;)]</annotation></semantics></math> for the Riesz representative of the (under mild conditions) bounded linear form <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mapsto\mathbb{E}_{x}[\langle f,k(x,\;\cdot\;)\rangle]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.280em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mapsto\mathbb{E}_{x}[\langle f,k(x,\;\cdot\;)\rangle]</annotation></semantics></math>, one may show:</p>
<table id="A4.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{MMD}(P,Q,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MMD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{MMD}(P,Q,k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[k(x,\;\cdot\&gt;)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[k(y,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\|_{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[k(x,\;\cdot\&gt;)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[k(y,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\|_{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{\|D\|_{\mathcal{H}}\leq 1}\left\langle D\,,\,\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[k(x,\;\cdot\&gt;)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[k(y,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{\|D\|_{\mathcal{H}}\leq 1}\left\langle D\,,\,\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[k(x,\;\cdot\&gt;)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[k(y,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E3Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E3Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{\|D\|_{\mathcal{H}}\leq 1}\left\langle D\,,\,\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[k(x,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\right\rangle-\left\langle D\,,\,\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[k(y,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\right\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.220em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{\|D\|_{\mathcal{H}}\leq 1}\left\langle D\,,\,\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[k(x,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\right\rangle-\left\langle D\,,\,\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[k(y,\;\cdot\&gt;)]\right\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E3Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E3Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{\|D\|_{\mathcal{H}}\leq 1}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[D(y)]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{\|D\|_{\mathcal{H}}\leq 1}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[D(y)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality in the second equality, the linearity of the inner product in the third equality, and the definition of the Riesz representative in the last equality.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This framing reveals that MMD is precisely the optimal linear classification risk achievable by a discriminator <math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in the unit ball of the function space induced by the kernel.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Background on <math id="A5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-Divergences and Total Variation Distance</h3>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be probability measures on <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> that are assumed to be absolutely continuous with respect to a base measure <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, having densities <math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
For now, also assume <math id="A5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to be absolutely continuous w.r.t. <math id="A5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. For a convex, lower-semicontinuous function <math id="A5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}_{+}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}_{+}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="A5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence of <math id="A5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{f}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q):=\int_{\mathcal{X}}q(x)f\left(\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}\right)d\mu" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q):=\int_{\mathcal{X}}q(x)f\left(\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}\right)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As shown by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2010)</a></cite>, <math id="A5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence can be estimated via a variational objective similar to that of MMD.
Using the Fenchel conjugate <math id="A5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(v):=\sup_{u\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}uv-f(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(v):=\sup_{u\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}uv-f(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence is lower-bounded by:</p>
<table id="A5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{f}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)\geq\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[f^{*}(D(x))]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)\geq\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[f^{*}(D(x))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for any family <math id="A5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of measurable functions <math id="A5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The bound is tight if and only if the functional class <math id="A5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently expressive to contain a subderivative of <math id="A5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at the density ratio <math id="A5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)/q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)/q(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a function then achieves the supremum. The variational formulation of the Jensen-Shannon divergence in Eqn. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.2 Variational Estimation of the Jensen Shannon Distance ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is a special case of Eqn. (<a href="#A5.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Appendix E Background on 𝑓-Divergences and Total Variation Distance ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Total Variation Distance.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The total variation (TV) distance corresponds to <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\frac{1}{2}|1-x|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\frac{1}{2}|1-x|</annotation></semantics></math>. One may easily verify that the integral in Eqn. (<a href="#A5.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Appendix E Background on 𝑓-Divergences and Total Variation Distance ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) evaluates to half of the <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. As we show in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Variational Formulation of TV Distance ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>, its variational objective in Eqn. (<a href="#A5.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Appendix E Background on 𝑓-Divergences and Total Variation Distance ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) can be reduced to:</p>
<table id="A5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}D:\mathcal{X}\to[0,1]\\
\gamma\in[0,1]\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}\left[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]\right]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}D:\mathcal{X}\to[0,1]\\
\gamma\in[0,1]\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}\left[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]\right]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we use the Iverson bracket <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[D(x)&gt;\gamma]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[D(x)&gt;\gamma]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the binarization of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> at the threshold <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. This objective is also known as the Informedness (or Youden’s J statistic) of the discriminator <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. It has a clear geometric interpretation as the maximum vertical distance between the ROC curve of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and the chance diagonal, with a fixed scale of <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances</h3>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose an alternative variant of the PGD, using variational estimates of the total variation (TV) distance in place of the Jensen-Shannon distance. We term this variant PGD-TV.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We recall from <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Background on 𝑓-Divergences and Total Variation Distance ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> that the variational objective for the TV distance is given by the informedness of a dichotomized classifier. We provide a proof of this fact in <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Variational Formulation of TV Distance ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>. When computing PGD-TV, the choice of binarization threshold is considered part of the fitting process of the classifier. Hence, we choose <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize the vertical distance of the ROC on the <em id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fit</em> set. We refer to <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B PGD Pseudocode ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for pseudocode.
In <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 PGD-TV Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a> and <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 PGD-TV Correlates With Model Quality ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>, we present an empirical investigation of PGD-TV, analogous to the experiments presented in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 PolyGraph Discrepancy Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 PolyGraph Discrepancy Correlates With Model Quality ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. Finally, we discuss the advantages of PGD over PGD-TV in <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Comparison of PGD and PGD-TV ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a></p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Variational Formulation of TV Distance</h4>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One may easily verify that for <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(u)=\frac{1}{2}|1-u|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(u)=\frac{1}{2}|1-u|</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following Fenchel conjugate:</p>
<table id="A6.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(v)=\sup_{u\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}uv-\frac{1}{2}|1-u|=\begin{cases}-\frac{1}{2}\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad v&lt;-\frac{1}{2}\\
v\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad v\in[-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}]\\
\infty&amp;\text{if}\quad v&gt;\frac{1}{2}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(v)=\sup_{u\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}uv-\frac{1}{2}|1-u|=\begin{cases}-\frac{1}{2}\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad v&lt;-\frac{1}{2}\\
v\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad v\in[-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}]\\
\infty&amp;\text{if}\quad v&gt;\frac{1}{2}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We recall the variational lower bound:</p>
<table id="A6.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)\geq\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[f^{*}(D(x))]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)\geq\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}[f^{*}(D(x))]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Without weakening the lower bound, we may restrict ourselves to families of functions which are upper-bounded by <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> almost everywhere w.r.t. <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, discriminators <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that do not satisfy this have a variational bound of <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we are assuming <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\ll Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\ll Q</annotation></semantics></math>, the discriminators are then also upper-bounded almost everywhere w.r.t. <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, w.l.o.g., we may assume that they are upper-bounded by <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> everywhere.
Under these assumptions, we obtain the simpler formulation:</p>
<table id="A6.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}\left[\max\left(D(x),-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{y\sim Q}\left[\max\left(D(x),-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\int_{\mathcal{X}}D(x)p(x)-\max\left(D(x),-\frac{1}{2}\right)q(x)d\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{D\in\mathcal{F}}\int_{\mathcal{X}}D(x)p(x)-\max\left(D(x),-\frac{1}{2}\right)q(x)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Under the constraint that <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)\leq\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)\leq\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may maximize the expression above in a pointwise fashion by:</p>
<table id="A6.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad p(x)&gt;q(x)\\
-\frac{1}{2}\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad p(x)\leq q(x)\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad p(x)&gt;q(x)\\
-\frac{1}{2}\qquad&amp;\text{if}\quad p(x)\leq q(x)\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">We note that this is consistent with the finding of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2010)</a></cite> that <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)</annotation></semantics></math> should attain a subderivative of <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at the point <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, without weakening the lower bound, we may write:</p>
<table id="A6.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\left\{-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}\left[\max\left(D(x),-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>max</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\left\{-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}\left[\max\left(D(x),-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\left\{-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[D(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\left\{-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[D(x)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E11Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E11Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\left\{0,1\right\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[D(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\left\{0,1\right\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[D(x)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E11Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E11Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}D:\mathcal{X}\to[0,1]\\
\gamma\in[0,1]\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}D:\mathcal{X}\to[0,1]\\
\gamma\in[0,1]\end{subarray}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[[D(x)&gt;\gamma]]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">The first equality is derived from the observation that <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)\geq-\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)\geq-\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> always holds and the maximum is therefore redundant. The second equality is obtained by noting that the expression is invariant under the addition of constants to <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> (in this case, we add <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Without relying on the results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2010)</a></cite>, we now show that this bound is tight, even when <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\not\ll Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪̸</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\not\ll Q</annotation></semantics></math>. To work in this more general setting, we redefine the total variation distance as half the <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance of <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A6.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q):=\frac{1}{2}\|p-q\|_{L^{1}(\mathcal{X},\mu)}=\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathcal{X}}|p(x)-q(x)|d\mu" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q):=\frac{1}{2}\|p-q\|_{L^{1}(\mathcal{X},\mu)}=\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathcal{X}}|p(x)-q(x)|d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">One may verify that this matches our original definition when <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\ll Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\ll Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any measurable set <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we note that:</p>
<table id="A6.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\int_{A}p(x)d\mu+\int_{A^{C}}p(x)d\mu=\int_{A}q(x)d\mu+\int_{A^{C}}q(x)d\mu" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\int_{A}p(x)d\mu+\int_{A^{C}}p(x)d\mu=\int_{A}q(x)d\mu+\int_{A^{C}}q(x)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Hence, rearranging, we obtain:</p>
<table id="A6.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{A}p(x)-q(x)d\mu=\int_{A^{C}}q(x)-p(x)d\mu" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{A}p(x)-q(x)d\mu=\int_{A^{C}}q(x)-p(x)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Defining <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\&gt;:\&gt;p(x)\geq q(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.498em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=\{x\in\mathcal{X}\&gt;:\&gt;p(x)\geq q(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and applying this identity, we get:</p>
<table id="A6.E15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E15X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathcal{X}}|p(x)-q(x)|d\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathcal{X}}|p(x)-q(x)|d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\int_{A}p(x)-q(x)d\mu+\frac{1}{2}\int_{A^{C}}q(x)-p(x)d\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>A</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\int_{A}p(x)-q(x)d\mu+\frac{1}{2}\int_{A^{C}}q(x)-p(x)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E15Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{A}p(x)-q(x)d\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>A</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{A}p(x)-q(x)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the set on which <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)-q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)-q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-negative, it is also clear that for any other <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="A6.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathcal{X}}|p(x)-q(x)|d\mu\geq\int_{B}p(x)-q(x)d\mu" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathcal{X}}|p(x)-q(x)|d\mu\geq\int_{B}p(x)-q(x)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">Thus, we may write:</p>
<table id="A6.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E17X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E17X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0.387em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{TV}(P\&gt;\|\&gt;Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E17X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{B\subset\mathcal{X}}\int_{B}p(x)-q(x)d\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>B</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{B\subset\mathcal{X}}\int_{B}p(x)-q(x)d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E17Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E17Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}}\int_{\mathcal{X}}D(x)(p(x)-q(x))d\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}}\int_{\mathcal{X}}D(x)(p(x)-q(x))d\mu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E17Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E17Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[D(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{D:\mathcal{X}\to\{0,1\}}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim P}[D(x)]-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim Q}[D(x)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.7" class="ltx_p">This is exactly the variational lower bound which we have derived above. Hence, we have shown it to be tight, even in the setting where <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\not\ll Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪̸</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\not\ll Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>PGD-TV Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations</h4>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now present perturbation experiments for the PGD-TV variant that are analogous to those shown in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 PolyGraph Discrepancy Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We plot a summary of the Spearman correlation of the metrics with perturbation magnitude in <a href="#A6.F6" title="In F.2 PGD-TV Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Compared to <a href="#S5.F3" title="In 5.2 PolyGraph Discrepancy Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we find that PGD-TV exhibits slightly lower correlations. <a href="#A6.F7" title="In F.2 PGD-TV Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#A6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ F.2 PGD-TV Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> show the response of PGD-TV to perturbation over the entire and cropped magnitude range, respectively. For a more detailed explanation of this type of plot, we refer to <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Supplemental for: PGD Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.
From the plots we conclude that PGD-TV qualitatively exhibits the expected behavior of increasing with perturbation magnitude and eventually saturating. However, in some cases (e.g., edge addition on proteins), the PGD-TV flattens out, leading to lower correlations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06122v1/correlation_plots_informedness_tabpfn_cropped.svg" id="A6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/98;" width="521" height="98"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Spearman correlation of MMD metrics and PGD-TV with the magnitude of perturbation of datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06122v1/metrics_vs_noise_level_informedness_tabpfn_full.png" id="A6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:543/579;" width="543" height="579" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Behavior of descriptor-specific and aggregated PolyGraph Discrepancy-TV as data distributions are perturbed. The perturbation type varies across rows while dataset varies across columns. The Spearman correlation of the aggregate PolyGraph Discrepancy and the perturbation level is denoted by <math id="A6.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06122v1/metrics_vs_noise_level_informedness_tabpfn_cropped.png" id="A6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:543/579;" width="543" height="579" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Behavior of descriptor-specific and aggregated PolyGraph Discrepancy-TV as data distributions are perturbed. The perturbation type varies across rows, while the dataset varies across columns. The Spearman correlation of the aggregate PolyGraph Discrepancy and the perturbation level is denoted by <math id="A6.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>PGD-TV Correlates With Model Quality</h4>

<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Analogous to <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 PolyGraph Discrepancy Correlates With Model Quality ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, we now investigate how the PGD-TV variant correlates with proxy variables of model quality.
In <a href="#A6.F9" title="In F.3 PGD-TV Correlates With Model Quality ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we illustrate how PGD-TV behaves as the number of denoising steps in DiGress is varied.
As in <a href="#S5.F4" title="In 5.2 PolyGraph Discrepancy Tracks Synthetic Data Perturbations ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we find that PGD-TV correlates with validity in a highly linear fashion.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As in <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 PolyGraph Discrepancy Correlates With Model Quality ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, we compute Pearson correlation coefficients between PGD-TV and validity. When varying the number of denoising steps, we find that PGD-TV exhibits a more linear relationship with validity than any of the MMD metrics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06122v1/model-quality_denoising-iterations_validity_polyscore_all_mmd_informedness-adaptive.svg" id="A6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/115;" width="521" height="115"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Behavior of validity, PolyGraph Discrepancy-TV, and MMDs as the number of denoising steps in DiGress is varied on <span id="A6.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Planar-L</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We examine the behavior of PGD-TV throughout training in <a href="#A6.F9" title="In F.3 PGD-TV Correlates With Model Quality ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. Qualitatively, a clear positive relationship emerges between training duration and PGD-TV. This trend is confirmed quantitatively in <a href="#A6.T8" title="In F.3 PGD-TV Correlates With Model Quality ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, where Spearman correlation coefficients show that most MMD metrics often exhibit weak or negative correlations, while PGD-TV consistently correlates positively with training duration. However, this correlation is weaker than that of PGD-JS (see <a href="#S5.T3" title="In 5.3 PolyGraph Discrepancy Correlates With Model Quality ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and the clustering-based MMD metric. A similar pattern appears in <a href="#A6.T7" title="In F.3 PGD-TV Correlates With Model Quality ‣ Appendix F PGD-TV: Estimating Total Variation Distances ‣ Appendix ‣ PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> (bottom three rows): PGD-TV correlates reliably with validity, whereas most MMD metrics show inconsistent behavior. Nevertheless, in two out of three cases, the clustering-based MMD metric achieves a stronger correlation with validity than PGD-TV.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06122v1/all_training_epochs_informedness-adaptive.svg" id="A6.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/314;" width="521" height="314"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Behavior of validity, PolyGraph Discrepancy-TV, and MMD metrics throughout training of DiGress on procedural graph datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
